+Virtio-msg Linux Bridge Internals
+=================================
+
+This document describes implementation details of the QEMU
+``virtio-msg-linux-bridge`` backend.
+
+For user-facing command-line usage, see:
+
+- :doc:`../system/devices/virtio/virtio-msg-linux-bridge`
+
+Scope
+-----
+
+The Linux bridge backend connects the ``virtio-msg`` transport core to the
+host kernel bridge UAPI (``/dev/virtio-msg-bridge``) using:
+
+- synchronous ioctls for endpoint resolution, attach, detach, and release
+- shared TX/RX rings for transport traffic and bridge-local runtime control
+- eventfds for kick/call notifications
+
+QOM Model
+---------
+
+The backend supports two compositions:
+
+- ``virtio-msg-linux-bridge-transport`` (recommended for CLI use)
+  A ``TYPE_SYS_BUS_DEVICE`` container that creates an outer bus, a
+  ``virtio-msg`` proxy, and an internal ``virtio-msg-linux-bridge``
+  backend. The transport parent fixes default ring geometry to
+  256 entries with 128-byte slots (the aligned ``VIRTIO_MSG_MAX_SIZE``
+  default) and passes that value as ``vmm-max-msg-size``.
+- ``virtio-msg-linux-bridge`` (standalone backend)
+  Attaches to an already existing ``virtio-msg`` object. Ring entry-size
+  validation is keyed to the backend's stored ``vmm-max-msg-size`` property.
+
+The backend itself derives from ``TYPE_VIRTIO_MSG_BUS_DEVICE`` and provides
+transport callbacks used by ``virtio-msg`` core.
+
+Realize and Attach Lifecycle
+----------------------------
+
+At realize time, the backend:
+
+1. Validates endpoint and ring geometry properties.
+2. Opens the bridge device node.
+3. Resolves endpoint string to a bridge endpoint handle
+   (``RESOLVE_F_CREATE`` only when ``create-endpoint=on``).
+4. Allocates shared ring backing and eventfds.
+5. Initializes the direct-map-only DMA address space and provisional transport
+   capabilities needed for command-line child device pre-plug.
+6. Marks the first host attach as pending.
+7. Installs a migration blocker.
+
+The first host attach is reset-exit work, after QEMU's initial cold reset. The
+backend issues ``ATTACH`` with ring/eventfd descriptors plus the attach-epoch
+``vmm_max_msg_size`` input, latches the attach snapshot (revision and feature
+bitmaps), seeds endpoint-scoped max-message state when the attach snapshot is
+online, registers the ``call_fd`` handler used to drain the RX ring and retry
+pending TX work, and, when the attached endpoint is already online, refreshes
+proxy transport capabilities and publishes topology.
+
+Unrealize performs the reverse sequence: unregister handlers, detach,
+release endpoint, unmap mapped areas, close fds, and remove the migration
+blocker.
+
+Teardown and Reset Rationale
+----------------------------
+
+Unrealize uses forced session teardown, not the ordinary deferred cleanup
+protocol. Runtime QEMU-initiated map release normally waits until the final map
+owner is gone, then sends ``MAP_RELEASED`` as a bridge-local event. Device
+unrealize cannot depend on that delayed protocol path: it must leave no live fd,
+handler, map object, or endpoint reference behind after the QOM object is torn
+down. The forced path therefore detaches the host session synchronously,
+invalidates delayed map-release completions, revokes all DMA-visible mappings,
+drops bridge-local protocol records, purges ring work, and then releases the
+endpoint and backing resources. ``MAP_RELEASED`` remains a runtime event, not a
+teardown fence.
+
+QEMU reset uses attach-epoch renewal semantics for the same ordering reason.
+The initial reset has no active host session yet: reset hold leaves the pending
+first attach in place, and reset exit issues the first ``ATTACH`` using the
+initialized rings and eventfds for the resolved endpoint. Later resets detach
+the active host bridge session during reset hold before dropping local ring,
+pending-TX, map, and bridge-local protocol state. Reset exit then reinitializes
+the rings, drains eventfds, and issues a new ``ATTACH``. This avoids keeping a
+host attach session active while QEMU has discarded the local state that
+describes that session, and it lets each attach epoch provide the authoritative
+online snapshot and topology publication state. This is QEMU backend policy
+layered above the Linux bridge UAPI; it does not add a Linux UAPI reset
+operation.
+
+Endpoint State Model
+--------------------
+
+Endpoint availability is driven by an explicit attach-session state machine:
+
+- ``DETACHED``: no active bridge attach
+- ``ATTACHED_OFFLINE``: attach succeeded, but endpoint-scoped topology, map,
+  RX, and TX work is quiesced for the current epoch
+- ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_NEEDS_TOPOLOGY``: the endpoint is online for the current
+  attach epoch, but transport traffic stays blocked until the full topology
+  snapshot has been published on the TX ring
+- ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_ACTIVE``: topology has been published successfully for the
+  current online epoch, so RX, TX, map receive, map responses, and pending-TX
+  flush are allowed
+- ``ATTACHED_FAILED``: terminal failed-attach state entered after unrecoverable
+  attach-session errors; data, map, and topology work do not resume until the
+  bridge is re-realized
+
+State transitions for this step are:
+
+- ``ATTACH`` snapshot online -> ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_NEEDS_TOPOLOGY``
+- ``ATTACH`` snapshot offline -> ``ATTACHED_OFFLINE``
+- complete topology snapshot published -> ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_ACTIVE``
+- ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` -> ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_NEEDS_TOPOLOGY``
+- ``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` -> ``ATTACHED_OFFLINE``
+- unrecoverable protocol, validation, or delivery failure -> ``ATTACHED_FAILED``
+
+If ``ATTACH`` reports the endpoint online, QEMU uses the attach snapshot as the
+initial online state. Linux does not send a synthetic ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` for
+that epoch, so QEMU enters ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_NEEDS_TOPOLOGY`` directly and
+publishes topology for ``caps.online_epoch`` once the machine is ready.
+
+``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` is a lifecycle refresh for a later online epoch: it updates
+endpoint-scoped fields (``bus_name`` and ``endpoint_max_msg_size``), clears
+old-epoch pending TX, pending bridge errors, map state, and live mappings, then
+requests topology republish. It is not a second attach-capability negotiation
+step.
+
+``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` is an attached-but-offline epoch transition. QEMU clears
+``caps.online_epoch`` to ``0``, clears pending TX and map operation state,
+revokes live bridge mappings, marks transport state offline, and resets
+attached child devices. The reset is QEMU transport policy, not a Linux bridge
+ABI requirement.
+
+Old-epoch lifecycle and map messages are stale and ignored. Apart from a valid
+later ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` that brings an offline session online, bridge-local
+messages whose epoch is ahead of the current online epoch are protocol
+violations and fail the attach. While offline or waiting for topology, the
+``call_fd`` handler still drains the RX ring so lifecycle messages can move the
+session forward, but endpoint-scoped transport, map, and pending
+required-delivery work remain blocked until ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_ACTIVE``.
+
+Bridge-Local Ring Contract
+--------------------------
+
+Runtime bridge control uses implementation-defined bus messages on the shared
+rings. There is no runtime ``dev_fd`` sideband control path.
+
+Allowed RX bridge-local messages are:
+
+- ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE``
+- ``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE``
+- ``MAP_ADD``
+- ``MAP_DEL``
+
+Allowed TX bridge-local messages are:
+
+- ``TOPOLOGY_RESET``
+- ``TOPOLOGY_DEV_ADD``
+- ``TOPOLOGY_COMMIT``
+- ``MAP_ADD``
+- ``MAP_DEL``
+- ``MAP_RELEASED``
+- ``BRIDGE_ERROR``
+
+Bridge-local messages use bus-class headers with ``dev_num == 0``. All
+bridge-local messages except map requests/responses and ``BRIDGE_ERROR`` use
+``token == 0``. Linux sends ``MAP_ADD`` and ``MAP_DEL`` as request-class bus
+messages with non-zero request tokens. QEMU copies each request token into the
+matching response-class ``MAP_ADD`` or ``MAP_DEL`` response. ``MAP_RELEASED``
+is a QEMU-to-Linux event, not a response, and uses ``token == 0``.
+``BRIDGE_ERROR`` is also a response-class bus message and copies the original
+transport request token for correlation.
+
+QEMU validates bridge-local direction, type bits, ``dev_num``, token, payload
+size, and current state before applying a message. Malformed bridge-local
+headers or payload sizes fault the attach. Standardized bus messages and
+reserved bus-message IDs are not part of the bridge ring protocol; QEMU
+discards them instead of forwarding them to generic ``virtio-msg`` provider
+paths.
+
+Data Path Contract
+------------------
+
+Data traffic uses shared TX/RX ring headers with monotonic ``u32`` cursors:
+
+- slot index: ``cursor % entries``
+- occupancy: ``prod - cons`` (unsigned arithmetic)
+- valid occupancy range: ``0..entries``
+
+Producer/consumer ordering follows acquire/release semantics:
+
+- producer publishes entry contents before advancing ``prod``
+- consumer reads ``prod`` with acquire semantics before reading payload
+- consumer advances ``cons`` only after processing is complete
+
+Occupancy above ``entries`` is treated as ring corruption and rejected.
+
+Copy-path behavior:
+
+- RX copies only the message header first, validates ``msg_size``, then copies
+  only validated payload bytes
+- TX copies only ``msg_size`` bytes to the ring slot (no full-slot clear)
+
+RX ring processing is strictly ordered, but the pinned slot depends on the
+pending output. ``MAP_ADD`` and ``MAP_DEL`` slots are consumed after QEMU
+records any deferred map work; if the matching response cannot be completed
+immediately, later map and transport RX work stays blocked while the deferred
+map state remains set. ``BRIDGE_ERROR`` backpressure pins the failed request
+slot only until the pending error report reaches the TX ring.
+
+Ring Geometry and Validation
+----------------------------
+
+Bridge-backed ring policy is split between transport defaults and backend
+validation:
+
+- transport parent defaults are fixed to 256 entries and 128-byte slots for
+  both TX and RX
+- backend validation requires exactly 256 entries on both rings and slot size
+  ``== ALIGN(vmm_max_msg_size, 128)``
+- realize-time ring validation uses the backend's stored
+  ``vmm-max-msg-size`` property for both TX and RX
+- for the current bridge UAPI this means transport-mode geometry is
+  effectively fixed to 256x128 rather than a generic runtime sizing knob
+- endpoint ``max_msg_size`` is learned per attach epoch from ``ATTACH`` and
+  later ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` lifecycle refresh payloads
+
+Required Delivery Contract
+--------------------------
+
+Required traffic uses ``send_must_deliver`` semantics:
+
+- synchronous responses and runtime notifications are queued when transient
+  TX-ring backpressure occurs
+- if a queued entry already exists, later required traffic is appended behind
+  it so transport-message order is preserved
+- queued traffic is retried via a bounded timer-backed backoff only while the
+  session is in ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_ACTIVE``
+- the retry delay starts at 1 ms, doubles after failed retries, and is capped
+  at 200 ms
+- each queued entry expires after 8 failed retry attempts
+- existing bridge progress points also attempt to flush queued traffic when the
+  session can make TX progress again
+- retry is canceled when the queue drains or the session leaves the active
+  online state
+- ``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` clears queued pending-TX entries for the old epoch
+- no busy-loop retry path is used
+
+Required-delivery expiry is message-class dependent:
+
+- queued responses and device runtime notifications
+  (``EVENT_CONFIG``, ``EVENT_AVAIL``, and ``EVENT_USED``) fail the attach when
+  they expire, because the bridge cannot preserve the required completion
+  contract after dropping them
+- bus-class traffic is never transmitted by the bridge backend; if such a
+  queued entry were encountered it would be dropped rather than used to fail
+  the attach
+- other queued entries that fail with non-retry errors are dropped after the
+  failure is logged
+
+Request errors before a required response is accepted are reported with a
+TX-ring ``BRIDGE_ERROR`` message. The report carries the original request
+``dev_num``, ``token``, ``msg_id``, and a signed negative errno value derived
+from the ``virtio-msg`` handler result. If TX-ring backpressure prevents
+immediate publication, QEMU records the pending error report and keeps the
+failed request slot pinned only until that report is published; the original
+request is not re-dispatched afterward. Terminal error-report failures fail the
+attach. Once a response has been accepted into the pending-TX queue, later
+bounded-delivery expiry is recovered by the attach-failure path rather than by
+synthesizing a second response.
+
+Runtime Notification Authority
+------------------------------
+
+Runtime notifications have exactly one authoritative transport-message
+occurrence:
+
+- QEMU device notifications are converted by the ``virtio-msg`` proxy into the
+  corresponding ``EVENT_USED`` or ``EVENT_CONFIG`` message
+- the Linux bridge backend forwards that message to the bridge TX ring and
+  does not synthesize an additional runtime notification from eventfds or
+  lifecycle messages
+- ``EVENT_AVAIL`` is received from the bridge RX ring and delivered to the
+  QEMU device model as the authoritative guest-available notification for that
+  queue
+
+Out-of-band fds are wakeup hints only:
+
+- ``call_fd`` wakes QEMU so it can drain RX ring work and retry pending TX;
+  draining an eventfd does not itself create a virtio-msg notification
+- ``kick_fd`` wakes the host side after QEMU publishes a TX-ring entry; the
+  TX-ring message is still the authoritative notification
+- polling paths remain transparent because polling only causes normal ring and
+  retry queues to be drained, and does not manufacture notifications outside
+  those queues
+
+Standard ioreq/ioeventfd notification acceleration is not reused here because
+virtio-msg queue notification is carried in-band as transport messages. There
+is no emulated MMIO or PIO notify access for KVM, Xen, or QEMU memory
+listeners to trap and bind to an ioeventfd.
+
+If bounded delivery expires for a required one-way runtime notification, the
+backend fails the attach. That recovery path moves the session to
+``ATTACHED_FAILED``, clears pending TX, purges queued RX, clears map operation
+state, revokes bridge mappings, detaches from the bridge, and releases the
+endpoint.
+
+Queue Address Preparation
+-------------------------
+
+``SET_VQUEUE`` configures queue size, vector, and ring addresses.
+
+Descriptor payload mappings, indirect descriptor caches, and vring caches use
+the bridge ``dma_as`` backed by bridge-map-installed RAM subregions.
+
+Bridge-driven cache lifecycle:
+
+- queue region caches are invalidated after successful map add/del, endpoint
+  offline, and bridge teardown/reset paths
+- ``EVENT_AVAIL`` rebuilds queue caches only when invalid
+- coverage misses while offline fail fast without polling ring progress
+
+DMA Mapping Model (Bridge-Local Map Messages + Pointer-Backed RAM)
+------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+The backend map table is populated by bridge-local ``MAP_ADD`` and
+``MAP_DEL`` request messages from the RX ring:
+
+The final DMA-sharing path is entirely bridge-managed: map messages publish and
+revoke generic DMA windows, and the transport does not use a ``GET_SHM`` bus
+message to publish them.
+The bridge DMA ``AddressSpace`` is private to the backend and configured
+direct-map-only; bridge ``MAP_ADD`` subregions are the only DMA-visible RAM
+windows in that address space.
+Because child virtio devices use this private DMA address space rather than
+the system memory address space, the bridge always exposes
+``VIRTIO_F_IOMMU_PLATFORM`` to make DMA translations go through the transport
+``AddressSpace``.
+
+1. ``MAP_ADD`` validates the bridge-local request payload, accepts
+   ``mmap_length >= length``, maps ``mmap_length`` bytes at ``mmap_offset``
+   from the attached bridge fd, creates one per-map QOM object for the
+   publication instance, and installs a pointer-backed RAM subregion into the
+   bridge ``dma_mr`` at ``bus_addr`` for exactly ``length`` bytes.
+2. The per-map object owns the map identity, lifecycle state, ``MemoryRegion``,
+   and host mapping. Generic DMA users see ordinary pointer-backed RAM through
+   the bridge ``dma_as``. The final ``munmap()`` is performed by the map
+   object's finalizer after the last owner reference is released.
+3. ``MAP_ADD`` response uses the same ``msg_id`` and token as the Linux
+   request and carries ``online_epoch``, ``map_seq``, ``map_id``, and a
+   signed negative errno status. A zero status is published only after
+   ``mmap()``, map-object creation, subregion installation, and map-cache
+   touch complete.
+4. ``MAP_DEL`` requests revoke-first teardown. QEMU first removes the
+   subregion from the bridge DMA ``AddressSpace`` so no new DMA lookup can
+   create a translation for that publication instance, then publishes the
+   matching ``MAP_DEL`` response. The response means DMA visibility has been
+   revoked; it does not wait for final map owner release or final
+   ``munmap()``.
+5. ``MAP_RELEASED`` is a QEMU-to-Linux event, not a response. QEMU sends it
+   after QEMU-initiated release has revoked DMA visibility and the final map
+   owner release has completed the final ``munmap()``. It carries
+   ``online_epoch``, ``map_seq``, ``map_id``, ``bus_addr``, and ``length`` with
+   ``token == 0``.
+
+Linux may publish multiple map requests before earlier responses have
+completed. QEMU consumes map requests in RX order and sends matching responses
+in that order. If QEMU cannot complete a map operation immediately, the RX
+slot is consumed after recording deferred map state, but later map RX slots and
+transport RX delivery are blocked until the delayed response or event is
+handled. Transport TX and required-delivery flush work are gated by the
+attach/data state and pending-TX queue state rather than by deferred map state.
+Map responses are sent through the same required-delivery TX path as other
+bridge-local completions.
+
+A non-zero ``MAP_ADD`` failure response is fatal to the QEMU attach after the
+response reaches Linux. A non-zero ``MAP_DEL`` status is reported in the
+matching response and consumes the request; QEMU fails the attach only if the
+required-delivery path cannot deliver that response. Transient ``MAP_ADD``
+setup failures are handled by a bounded internal timer-backed attempt loop
+before QEMU publishes a final response.
+
+``VIRTIO_MSG_BUS_BRIDGE_MAP_F_RETENTION_REQUESTED`` is a Linux hint that
+retaining an idle mapping may avoid later ``mmap()`` and setup cost. It is not
+a hard promise from QEMU. Non-retained temporary mappings are the normal
+candidates for QEMU-initiated proactive release. Retained mappings are
+protected from ordinary proactive reclaim, but explicit ``MAP_DEL`` and
+exceptional cleanup paths such as fault, reset, detach, teardown, or
+exceptional pressure may still release them. If QEMU releases a retained
+mapping while the affected virtio device is still active, Linux removes that
+device rather than treating ``MAP_RELEASED`` as ordinary retained-map cleanup.
+
+Ordinary proactive release of non-retained maps is a bridge-local policy. QEMU
+schedules a reclaim sweep after an actual transport-visible ``EVENT_USED``
+frame has been copied to the bridge TX ring and the producer cursor has been
+published. The sweep skips retained maps and maps already being deleted or
+released, then releases at most one eligible map: the candidate with the
+oldest ``last_use_seq``. Count and byte thresholds schedule the same sweep as
+additional bounds. The current pressure thresholds are 256 live non-retained
+maps or 64 MiB of non-retained mapped bytes; after one release, QEMU chains
+another reclaim BH only while one of those thresholds is still met. Endpoint
+reset, detach, and teardown paths revoke or release remaining mappings even if
+no ``EVENT_USED`` is emitted.
+
+When QEMU sends ``MAP_RELEASED`` before Linux's matching ``MAP_DEL`` crosses on
+the ring, QEMU keeps a tombstone for the released identity and span. The
+tombstone blocks overlapping replacement maps, lets the crossing ``MAP_DEL``
+retire the record with a successful response, and prevents ABA reuse of the
+same ``online_epoch``/``map_seq``/``map_id`` tuple.
+
+Map entry identity and ownership are bridge-local:
+
+- one QOM map object owns each accepted ``MAP_ADD`` publication instance
+- the map list records live, revoking, finalized, release-pending, and
+  tombstone states
+- the map object's owner references keep the host mapping alive until generic
+  memory users and delayed completion paths have dropped their references
+- final owner release runs the map finalizer, performs ``munmap()``, and
+  schedules the delayed ``MAP_RELEASED`` event when QEMU initiated the release
+- ``map_lock`` protects map-list lookup, overlap checks, lifecycle transitions,
+  tombstones, and reclaim counters; it is not a generic RAM access refcount
+- map add/del contracts require page-aligned exact spans and non-zero lengths
+  (zero bus addresses are accepted)
+- ``MAP_ADD`` with unsupported flags or nonzero reserved fields receives a
+  failed ``MAP_ADD`` response
+- ``MAP_DEL`` with nonzero reserved fields receives a failed ``MAP_DEL``
+  response
+
+Lookup optimization:
+
+- a single-entry covering-map cache is checked before QTAILQ scan
+- each hit/install updates ``last_use_seq`` through
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_touch_locked()``
+- cache is invalidated on entry removal, full unmap, and lifecycle resets
+- linear map-list lookup is acceptable for this backend because active
+  non-retained maps are expected to remain small and are currently bounded by
+  reclaim thresholds of 256 live non-retained maps or 64 MiB of non-retained
+  mapped bytes
+
+Lifetime rules:
+
+- no opportunistic release is performed for cache pressure
+- DMA visibility ends on ``MAP_DEL``, QEMU-initiated release of non-retained
+  temporary mappings, endpoint offline, detach, bridge teardown, exceptional
+  pressure, or other exceptional retained cleanup
+- final host mapping lifetime ends only after the map object finalizer runs and
+  performs ``munmap()``
+- teardown revokes any remaining live mappings and releases bridge metadata
+- ``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` also clears any pending map operation state for the old
+  epoch
+- stale old-epoch ``MAP_ADD`` messages are consumed without a response
+- stale old-epoch ``MAP_DEL`` messages are consumed without a response unless
+  they retire a matching tombstone, in which case QEMU sends the matching
+  ``MAP_DEL`` response
+- future-epoch map messages fault the attach
+
+Linux-side UAPI documentation follow-up: the imported
+``linux-headers/linux/virtio_msg_bus_bridge.h`` interface does not require a
+structure change for this model. Linux-side comments or companion
+documentation should define successful ``MAP_DEL`` completion as DMA
+visibility revocation with no new translations for the publication instance,
+not as proof that userspace has already called ``munmap()``. They should also
+describe ``MAP_RELEASED`` as delayed until final owner release and final
+``munmap()``.
+
+Capability Latching
+-------------------
+
+Transport capability selection is latched from the attach snapshot:
+
+- the bridge backend accepts only transport revision ``1`` (the
+  ``VIRTIO_MSG_TRANSPORT_REVISION`` constant)
+- the attach snapshot provides the authoritative transport and bridge feature
+  bitmaps for the attach epoch
+- advertised transport features are carried from the attach snapshot, except
+  that ``VIRTIO_MSG_F_STRICT_CONFIG_GENERATION`` is masked out during this
+  phase
+- ``VIRTIO_F_NOTIF_CONFIG_DATA`` and ``VIRTIO_F_NOTIFICATION_DATA`` are also
+  masked because the current event encoding cannot preserve notification-data
+  semantics; support requires a future event encoding that carries those
+  semantics explicitly
+- extra features exported to child devices are fixed when the transport caps
+  are latched and include ``VIRTIO_F_IOMMU_PLATFORM``
+- child pre-plug observes the current latched transport caps; later lifecycle
+  refreshes do not renegotiate attach-snapshot revision or feature ownership
+
+Capability validation and max-size derivation are split by source:
+
+- ``ATTACH`` validates the attach ``revision`` and normalizes
+  ``max_msg_size`` with ``0 -> VIRTIO_MSG_MAX_SIZE``
+- attach ``max_msg_size`` must be in the implemented v2 transport range
+  ``VIRTIO_MSG_MIN_SIZE..VIRTIO_MSG_MAX_SIZE`` (currently ``52..128`` bytes)
+- attach snapshots are the only source for revision, transport features, and
+  bridge features
+- attach snapshots seed endpoint-scoped max-message state only when the
+  endpoint is already online at attach time
+- ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` is a lifecycle refresh for endpoint data only: it
+  updates ``bus_name`` and endpoint ``max_msg_size`` after applying the same
+  ``0 -> VIRTIO_MSG_MAX_SIZE`` normalization and ``52..128`` validation
+- ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` does not update revision, transport features, or bridge
+  features
+- effective transport max is recomputed as
+  ``min(vmm_max_msg_size, endpoint_max_msg_size_when_online)``, with the
+  endpoint term absent while offline
+
+Topology Publication Model
+--------------------------
+
+Topology publication uses a full-snapshot TX ring model:
+
+- attach-online snapshots and later ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` transitions enter
+  ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_NEEDS_TOPOLOGY``
+- ``machine_done`` and ``ENDPOINT_ONLINE`` may publish topology only from
+  ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_NEEDS_TOPOLOGY``
+- QEMU publishes ``TOPOLOGY_RESET`` for ``caps.online_epoch``
+- QEMU publishes one ``TOPOLOGY_DEV_ADD`` for each populated external device
+  number, in ascending device-number order
+- QEMU publishes ``TOPOLOGY_COMMIT`` with the final device count
+- successful publication of the complete ordered snapshot is the only
+  transition into ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_ACTIVE``
+- TX-ring full conditions queue the unsent topology message and later topology
+  messages behind it; the session stays in ``ATTACHED_ONLINE_NEEDS_TOPOLOGY``
+  until the queued ``TOPOLOGY_COMMIT`` reaches the TX ring
+- terminal topology publication failures fail the attach
+- the published range is external device number ``0`` through
+  ``VIRTIO_MSG_MAX_DEVS - 1``
+- runtime child hotplug and unplug are unsupported
+- no runtime ``DEV_NUM_ADD``/``DEV_NUM_REMOVE`` publication path is kept
+- the ``virtio-msg`` proxy bus is intentionally not hotpluggable
+
+``ENDPOINT_OFFLINE`` is treated as an attached-but-offline epoch transition,
+not as an implicit detach. QEMU keeps the bridge session attached, clears
+pending-TX entries and retry state, clears pending map operation state, unmaps
+all bridge mappings, invalidates proxy caches, and resets attached child
+devices. The device reset is retained QEMU transport policy layered on top of
+the Linux bridge ABI rather than an ABI requirement.
+
+Concurrency Notes
+-----------------
+
+The backend's runtime wakeup path is intentionally narrow:
+
+- ``call_fd`` is the kernel-to-QEMU runtime progress notification
+- the callback drains the eventfd, tries to flush pending TX, processes the RX
+  ring, and tries another TX flush after RX work has produced response/error
+  output
+- the ``virtio-msg`` transport ``process`` callback follows the same
+  flush-RX-flush shape for polling-driven progress
+- the pending-TX retry timer also retries queued required traffic and pending
+  ``BRIDGE_ERROR`` reports
+
+The bridge device fd is retained for synchronous ioctls and ``mmap()`` only. It
+is not a runtime readiness or control channel.
+
+Migration
+---------
+
+Migration is explicitly blocked for this backend.
+
+Reason: bridge session state (resolved endpoint handle, attached fd state,
+map tokens, and host bridge lifecycle) is host-local and cannot be
+transparently reconstructed on destination.
+
+Tracing and Debugging
+---------------------
+
+Relevant diagnostics include:
+
+- guest-error logs for malformed bridge-local payloads, malformed ring state,
+  or endpoint mismatch
+- warning logs for short/failed eventfd writes and fd read errors
+- tracepoints covering RX/TX message flow, bridge-local RX/TX, endpoint
+  resolve/release, fd events, lifecycle transitions, and pending-TX
+  retries/drops
+- map lifecycle traces:
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_add_rx``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_del_rx``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_add_resp_tx``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_del_resp_tx``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_released_tx``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_event_install``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_event_remove``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_revoke``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_finalize``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_release_pending``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_tombstone``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_tombstone_del``,
+  ``virtio_msg_linux_bridge_map_event_stale``
